  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "y3xKgEzle/+ATpfhcmG0VVE5Hs0=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:29:19.010Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Exentra's Flowable listing describes the engine as embedded in its custom project-controlling software. The research classes the firm as a vendor rather than an integrator on that basis alone.\n\nWhat the listing therefore records is a build-time dependency inside a product, not a delivery practice serving third parties. That inverts the usual reasoning about tool adoption: where an integrator's platform choice is made per client engagement and can be revisited, an OEM's is a component decision inside a shipping product, owned by whoever owns the roadmap. The relationship a small vendor can hope for is displacement of a component, not attachment to a project.\n\nThe record is directory-thin beyond that. Unlike Avaloq, no headcount is recorded, no contact was resolved, and the row was never imprint-swept \u2014 it is the Flowable card and the inference drawn from it, mined on 2026-08-18.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**unprioritised**\n- Exentra GmbH (DE) \u2014 exentra.de\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> OEM PARTNER \u2014 embeds Flowable in custom project-controlling software. Vendor, not an integrator \u00b7 SOURCE: Flowable Partner Finder (flowable.com/company/partners), mined 2026-08-18. NOT imprint-swept.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:29:19.010658Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:29:19.010662Z"
    }
  },
